1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,741
This is STAT 331, video 22. It covers Lecture 12: multicollinearity.

2
00:00:07,191 --> 00:00:13,655
This video prepares you for three question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:14,105 --> 00:00:23,023
Type A: compute the variance inflation factor, the VIF, of one coefficient. Practice questions 1 and 2 a ask this.

4
00:00:23,473 --> 00:00:34,651
Type B: reason about VIFs. Show that a VIF is at least 1, and say what happens when we add a covariate, as in Practice question 2 b.

5
00:00:35,101 --> 00:00:42,056
Type C: use the idealized design matrix X star. Practice question 1 a and b ask this.

6
00:00:42,506 --> 00:00:57,461
Lecture 12 starts with a recap of Lecture 11. SSTotal is the sum of squared distances of y i from y bar. The full bar shows it for the real estate model that we use later in this video.

7
00:00:57,911 --> 00:01:09,324
SSReg is the part that the regression model explains. It is the sum of squared distances of the fitted values from y bar. The green part of the bar shows it.

8
00:01:09,774 --> 00:01:22,190
SSRes is the part that the model does not explain. It is the sum of the squared residuals. The orange part shows it. SSTotal is SSReg plus SSRes.

9
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:41,861
R squared is SSReg over SSTotal: 6769.1 over 9847.9, which is 0.6874. It is the proportion of the variability in y that the model explains.

10
00:01:42,311 --> 00:01:52,743
Type A uses the R squared of a regression of one covariate on the other covariates. The ANOVA table and the F tests are in video 21.

11
00:01:53,193 --> 00:02:11,113
Slide 13 is an aside: the geometry of least squares. The columns of X are vectors in n-dimensional space: the column of ones, x 1, up to x p. Their linear combinations form the column space of X. The blue plane shows it.

12
00:02:11,563 --> 00:02:19,883
The response vector y is also a vector in n-dimensional space. In general, y is not in the column space.

13
00:02:20,333 --> 00:02:29,656
Every vector X times b is in the column space. So the fitted vector, y hat equals X beta hat, is a point in the plane.

14
00:02:30,106 --> 00:02:40,538
The residual vector e is y minus y hat. It goes from the plane to y. Least squares chooses beta hat so that e is as short as possible.

15
00:02:40,988 --> 00:02:53,767
The shortest e is perpendicular to the plane, so it is perpendicular to every column of X. In symbols, X transpose e is 0. The next slide shows this with algebra.

16
00:02:54,217 --> 00:03:09,107
Slide 14 proves that X transpose e is 0. Line 1: e is y minus y hat, and y hat is H y. So e is I minus H, times y. The orange arrow in the picture is e.

17
00:03:09,557 --> 00:03:16,576
Line 2: multiply out the bracket. We get X transpose y, minus X transpose H y.

18
00:03:17,026 --> 00:03:28,119
Line 3: put in the hat matrix H, which is X, times X transpose X inverse, times X transpose. The picture marks H y, the fitted vector.

19
00:03:28,569 --> 00:03:36,719
Line 4: X transpose X, times its inverse, is the identity. So the middle term is X transpose y.

20
00:03:37,169 --> 00:03:48,710
Line 5: X transpose y minus X transpose y is 0. So e is perpendicular to every column of X, as the right angle in the picture shows.

21
00:03:49,160 --> 00:04:03,731
The result: y hat is the projection of y onto the column space of X. So y hat depends only on the column space, not on the columns that we choose to span it. Type C uses this in step 3.

22
00:04:04,181 --> 00:04:23,999
Slide 15. Suppose X has the columns 1, x 1, x 2 and x 3. Now suppose x 3 is a linear combination of the other columns: a 0 times 1, plus a 1 times x 1, plus a 2 times x 2. The table is our own small example.

23
00:04:24,449 --> 00:04:32,812
Build each value of x 3 as a stack. Start with the gray block, a 0 times 1, which is 1 in every row.

24
00:04:33,262 --> 00:04:35,417
Add x i 1, in blue.

25
00:04:35,867 --> 00:04:50,864
Add 2 times x i 2, in orange. The tops of the stacks are 2, 5, 4 and 7. These are exactly the values of x 3 in the table. So here a 0 is 1, a 1 is 1, and a 2 is 2.

26
00:04:51,314 --> 00:05:07,165
Then the columns of X are linearly dependent, and X transpose X shows it. Its column 4 is column 1, plus column 2, plus 2 times column 3. In row 1: 4 plus 10 plus 2 times 2 is 18.

27
00:05:07,615 --> 00:05:20,820
So the determinant of X transpose X is 0. The matrix has no inverse, and the least squares estimator beta hat cannot be computed. This is called perfect multicollinearity.

28
00:05:21,270 --> 00:05:32,193
Least squares requires no perfect multicollinearity. In words: x 3 cannot explain anything that x 1 and x 2 do not already explain.

29
00:05:32,643 --> 00:05:42,584
Slide 16 asks: what if a covariate is not exactly a linear combination of the other covariates, but only strongly correlated with them?

30
00:05:43,034 --> 00:05:54,149
Here is one data set from the simulation of the lecture. Each point is one subject, with its values of x 1 and x 2. The points lie close to a line.

31
00:05:54,599 --> 00:06:06,033
The correlation of x 1 and x 2 is 0.99. Correlation measures linear dependence, so x 2 is almost a linear function of x 1.

32
00:06:06,483 --> 00:06:14,633
The lecture runs a simulation in R. First it regresses y on x 1 only. We call this model M1.

33
00:06:15,083 --> 00:06:20,480
Then it regresses y on both x 1 and x 2. We call this model M2.

34
00:06:20,930 --> 00:06:25,261
The question is: what happens to beta 1 hat when we add x 2?

35
00:06:25,711 --> 00:06:41,839
Slides 17 and 18 give the R code of the simulation. Step 1 is the true model. beta 0 is 1, beta 1 is 2, and beta 2 is 0. The errors are normal with standard deviation 1.5.

36
00:06:42,289 --> 00:06:57,009
Step 2 generates the covariates. There are 100 subjects. x 1 and x 2 come from a multivariate normal distribution with mean 0, variance 1, and correlation 0.99.

37
00:06:57,459 --> 00:07:10,344
Step 3 fits both models to the same data: M1 with x 1 only, and M2 with x 1 and x 2. It stores beta 1 hat and its standard error from each model.

38
00:07:10,794 --> 00:07:26,410
The code repeats steps 2 and 3 for 1000 replicates. Then we compare the 1000 values of beta 1 hat from M1 with the 1000 values from M2. We ran the lecture code in R to get the results.

39
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:41,367
beta 2 is 0, so x 2 has no effect on y. So both models are correct models for y. The only difference is the extra covariate x 2, which is highly correlated with x 1.

40
00:07:41,817 --> 00:08:00,782
Here are the results. The green histogram shows the 1000 values of beta 1 hat from M1. In the table, their mean is 2.01, their standard deviation is 0.15, and the mean of the standard errors is also 0.15.

41
00:08:01,232 --> 00:08:15,099
The orange histogram shows beta 1 hat from M2. Its mean is 2.05, but its standard deviation is 1.05. The mean standard error is 1.08.

42
00:08:15,549 --> 00:08:26,066
Both histograms are centred close to the true value, beta 1 equals 2, the dashed purple line. So both estimators are close to unbiased.

43
00:08:26,516 --> 00:08:47,529
But M2 spreads much more. The bars under the plot show plus and minus one standard deviation. The standard error is 1.08 over 0.15, which is 7.2 times larger. Adding the correlated covariate x 2 inflates the standard error of beta 1 hat.

44
00:08:47,979 --> 00:09:09,569
Slide 19 gives the intuition: it is difficult to pull apart the variability explained by x 1 from the variability explained by x 2. Each orange point is beta 1 hat and beta 2 hat from M2 in one replicate. beta 1 hat goes from minus 1.32 to 5.46.

45
00:09:10,019 --> 00:09:26,808
The points lie along a line: beta 1 hat plus beta 2 hat equals 2. The correlation of the two estimates is minus 0.99. When beta 1 hat is too large, beta 2 hat is too small by almost the same amount.

46
00:09:27,258 --> 00:09:42,213
The green point is the truth: beta 1 is 2 and beta 2 is 0. It is on the same line. Because x 1 and x 2 are almost the same, the data fit almost equally well at every point on this line.

47
00:09:42,663 --> 00:09:48,636
So multicollinearity has two implications. First, the standard errors are inflated.

48
00:09:49,086 --> 00:09:59,411
Second, the estimates are unstable: a small change in the data moves beta 1 hat and beta 2 hat far, in opposite directions along the line.

49
00:09:59,861 --> 00:10:13,963
Slide 29 asks: what if we are interested in beta 1 plus beta 2? Recall the interpretation. It is the mean difference in y for a 1-unit increase in both x 1 and x 2.

50
00:10:14,413 --> 00:10:27,127
Slide 30 adds beta 1 hat plus beta 2 hat to the simulation. The orange histogram is beta 1 hat of M2 again. Its standard deviation is 1.05.

51
00:10:27,577 --> 00:10:46,137
The blue histogram is beta 1 hat plus beta 2 hat of M2. Its mean is 2.01. Its standard deviation and its mean standard error are both 0.15. The standard error uses the vector c equal to 0, 1, 1.

52
00:10:46,587 --> 00:10:59,814
The bars under the plot compare the two spreads. The sum has no variance inflation. Its standard error, 0.15, is the same as that of beta 1 hat in M1.

53
00:11:00,264 --> 00:11:21,277
Slide 31 explains. x 1 and x 2 are highly correlated. Changes in y track changes in x 1, but also changes in x 2. So we cannot pick apart the association with x 1 from the association with x 2. The points spread far along the purple line.

54
00:11:21,727 --> 00:11:38,111
beta 1 plus beta 2 is the change in mean y when both covariates increase by 1. It does not matter how this change splits between x 1 and x 2. The blue arrow shows this direction. The points spread very little in it.

55
00:11:38,561 --> 00:11:48,737
The slide says the same holds when we estimate a mean response or predict a new response. Practice question 1 b shows this with data.

56
00:11:49,187 --> 00:12:01,475
So be most concerned about multicollinearity when you interpret individual coefficients, beta j. Combinations that move the correlated covariates together are estimated well.

57
00:12:01,925 --> 00:12:15,323
How do we detect multicollinearity? Slide 20: for two covariates, look at the scatterplot of x 1 and x 2, and compute their correlation. Here the correlation is 0.99.

58
00:12:15,773 --> 00:12:31,623
Slide 21: for many covariates, look at the scatterplots and the correlation matrix of X. Here is the correlation matrix of the three real estate covariates. SqrFeet and Bedrooms have correlation 0.56.

59
00:12:32,073 --> 00:12:43,807
But a covariate can be moderately correlated with each other covariate, and still be close to a linear combination of all of them together. Pairwise correlations do not show this.

60
00:12:44,257 --> 00:12:50,337
So we look at all the other covariates together, with a regression. The next section does this.

61
00:12:50,787 --> 00:13:09,069
Slide 22. Treat x j as the outcome, and regress it on X minus j: the matrix of all the other covariates, with the column of ones. This is a multiple linear regression. alpha is its coefficient vector, and epsilon star is its error.

62
00:13:09,519 --> 00:13:32,282
Slide 23 recalls a fact. In simple linear regression, the squared correlation of y and x is R squared. In multiple linear regression, the squared correlation of y and the fitted values y hat is R squared. For the Practice Q1 model, both numbers are 0.6874.

63
00:13:32,732 --> 00:13:41,820
Example: regress SqrFeet on Bedrooms and Lotsize. The plot shows SqrFeet against its fitted value, x hat i 1.

64
00:13:42,270 --> 00:13:48,755
The correlation of x 1 and x hat 1 is 0.5690.

65
00:13:49,205 --> 00:14:01,941
Its square, 0.3238, is R 1 squared, the R squared of this regression. R j squared measures how well the other covariates together explain x j.

66
00:14:02,391 --> 00:14:16,343
Slide 25. In the simple linear regression of y on x j, the variance of the slope estimate is sigma squared, over the sum of squared distances of x i j from x bar j.

67
00:14:16,793 --> 00:14:33,775
In multiple linear regression, the variance of beta j hat is sigma squared times the j j entry of X transpose X inverse. The slide states that this equals the same fraction, times 1 over 1 minus R j squared.

68
00:14:34,225 --> 00:14:50,886
The factor 1 over 1 minus R j squared is the variance inflation factor, VIF j. The curve shows it as a function of R j squared. It starts at 1, and it grows without limit as R j squared goes to 1.

69
00:14:51,336 --> 00:15:11,304
For SqrFeet in the real estate model, R 1 squared is 0.3238. 1 minus 0.3238 is 0.6762, and 1 over 0.6762 is 1.479. The blue point shows it.

70
00:15:11,754 --> 00:15:39,509
In the simulation, x 1 on x 2 gives R squared close to 0.99 squared, which is 0.9801. 1 minus 0.9801 is 0.0199, so the VIF is 50.25. The standard error grows by the square root of the VIF, 7.09. This agrees with the 7.2 of the simulation.

71
00:15:39,959 --> 00:15:52,887
Slide 26 lists three properties. First, standard errors are larger when R j squared is larger. The curve goes up from left to right along the R j squared axis.

72
00:15:53,337 --> 00:16:06,158
Second, the VIF is at least 1: the curve never goes below the dashed line at 1. The slide asks why. This is a type B question, and we solve it after method card B.

73
00:16:06,608 --> 00:16:24,763
Third, a balance. A new covariate can increase R j squared, and so the VIF. But it can also explain more of the variability in y. Then the estimate of sigma squared gets smaller. Both changes act on the variance of beta j hat.

74
00:16:25,213 --> 00:16:39,293
Question type A asks for the VIF of one coefficient. The data come as a data file, as in Practice question 1, or as R output of other model fits, as in Practice question 2.

75
00:16:39,743 --> 00:16:45,993
Step 1 of method card A: pick x j, the covariate of the coefficient beta j.

76
00:16:46,443 --> 00:16:54,635
Step 2: find the regression of x j on all the other covariates. In this regression, x j is the outcome.

77
00:16:55,085 --> 00:17:02,061
Step 3: read R j squared. In R output, it is the Multiple R-squared of that regression.

78
00:17:02,511 --> 00:17:07,183
Step 4: the VIF is 1 over 1 minus R j squared.

79
00:17:07,633 --> 00:17:22,823
Step 5: say what it means. The variance of beta j hat is VIF j times the variance with an uncorrelated x j. The standard error is the square root of VIF j times larger.

80
00:17:23,273 --> 00:17:41,321
Now a practice question of type A. Pause the video and try it. Practice question 1 uses the real estate data with 521 houses. Fit sale price on SqrFeet, Bedrooms and Lotsize, and compute the VIF for beta 1.

81
00:17:41,771 --> 00:17:49,301
Step 1 of card A: beta 1 is the coefficient of SqrFeet. So x j is x 1, SqrFeet.

82
00:17:49,751 --> 00:18:02,146
Step 2: regress SqrFeet on the other two covariates, Bedrooms and Lotsize. SqrFeet is the outcome. We fit it to the 521 houses of the data file.

83
00:18:02,596 --> 00:18:08,548
Step 3: read R 1 squared. It is 0.3238.

84
00:18:08,998 --> 00:18:13,990
Step 4: VIF 1 is 1 over 1 minus R 1 squared.

85
00:18:14,440 --> 00:18:20,243
Put in R 1 squared from the table: 0.3238.

86
00:18:20,693 --> 00:18:27,029
1 minus 0.3238 is 0.6762.

87
00:18:27,479 --> 00:18:35,436
1 over 0.6762 is 1.479. The blue point on the curve shows it.

88
00:18:35,886 --> 00:18:55,299
Step 5: the variance of beta 1 hat is 1.479 times the variance with an uncorrelated SqrFeet. The standard error is the square root of 1.479, which is 1.216 times larger. This is a moderate inflation.

89
00:18:55,749 --> 00:19:21,264
Now check the formula of slide 25 with the same data. The base part is sigma hat squared over the sum of squared distances of SqrFeet from its mean. Sigma hat is 77,168.55, with 517 degrees of freedom. The sum is 263,407,931.

90
00:19:21,714 --> 00:19:26,727
Square sigma hat: 5.955 times 10 to the 9.

91
00:19:27,177 --> 00:19:33,343
Divide by the sum: the base part is 22.61. The blue bar shows it.

92
00:19:33,793 --> 00:19:45,889
The R output of the full model gives the standard error of beta 1 hat, 5.7821. Its square is 33.43. The bracket shows it.

93
00:19:46,339 --> 00:19:58,648
Divide: 33.43 over 22.61 is 1.479. This is VIF 1. The orange part is the extra variance.

94
00:19:59,098 --> 00:20:06,586
So the data agree with slide 25: the squared standard error is the base part times the VIF.

95
00:20:07,036 --> 00:20:23,975
Practice question 2 is type A with R output. Pause the video and try it. We fit y on x 1, x 2 and x 3, with 300 observations. Compute the VIFs for beta 1, beta 2 and beta 3.

96
00:20:24,425 --> 00:20:36,350
Steps 1 and 2 of card A for beta 1: the covariate is x 1. In the appendix, g 1 23 is the regression of x 1 on x 2 and x 3.

97
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:43,989
Step 3: its Multiple R-squared is R 1 squared, 0.2497.

98
00:20:44,439 --> 00:20:58,455
Step 4: VIF 1 is 1 over 1 minus 0.2497. That is 1 over 0.7503, which is 1.3328.

99
00:20:58,905 --> 00:21:20,025
Repeat steps 2 to 4 for beta 2. g 2 13 is the regression of x 2 on x 1 and x 3. R 2 squared is 0.7056, and VIF 2 is 1 over 0.2944, which is 3.3967.

100
00:21:20,475 --> 00:21:36,283
For beta 3, g 3 12 gives R 3 squared equal to 0.7107. VIF 3 is 1 over 0.2893, which is 3.4566.

101
00:21:36,733 --> 00:21:56,317
Step 5: the variances of beta 2 hat and beta 3 hat are about 3.4 times larger. Their standard errors are 1.84 and 1.86 times larger. Only the regressions with a covariate as the outcome give the R j squared values.

102
00:21:56,767 --> 00:22:12,597
Question type B asks you to reason about VIFs, with no data. Slide 26 asks why a VIF is at least 1. Practice question 2 b asks what happens to the VIFs when we add a covariate.

103
00:22:13,047 --> 00:22:20,236
Step 1 of method card B: write VIF j equals 1 over 1 minus R j squared.

104
00:22:20,686 --> 00:22:31,459
Step 2: find what the question says about R j squared. It can be its range, or a change in the regression of x j on the other covariates.

105
00:22:31,909 --> 00:22:44,944
Step 3: use the facts of R squared. R j squared is at least 0 and less than 1. One more covariate cannot increase SSRes, so R j squared cannot decrease.

106
00:22:45,394 --> 00:22:55,015
Step 4: 1 over 1 minus r increases with r. So conclude for the VIF and for the standard error of beta j hat.

107
00:22:55,465 --> 00:23:05,385
A type B question from slide 26. Pause the video and try it. Show that VIF j is at least 1. When is it exactly 1?

108
00:23:05,835 --> 00:23:12,299
Step 1 of card B: write VIF j equals 1 over 1 minus R j squared.

109
00:23:12,749 --> 00:23:19,021
Step 2: this question is about the range of R j squared, on the horizontal axis.

110
00:23:19,471 --> 00:23:36,175
Step 3: R squared is a proportion of SSTotal, so R j squared is at least 0. R j squared equal to 1 means perfect multicollinearity, which least squares excludes. So R j squared is less than 1.

111
00:23:36,625 --> 00:23:44,561
Multiply by minus 1 and add 1. Then 1 minus R j squared is greater than 0, and at most 1.

112
00:23:45,011 --> 00:23:58,430
Step 4: take 1 over this number. A number in the interval from 0 to 1 has an inverse of at least 1. So VIF j is at least 1. The curve is never below the dashed line.

113
00:23:58,880 --> 00:24:10,101
Equality: VIF j is 1 only when 1 minus R j squared is 1, that is, when R j squared is 0. The green point shows it.

114
00:24:10,551 --> 00:24:19,106
So VIF j is at least 1. It is exactly 1 only when x j is uncorrelated with the other covariates.

115
00:24:19,556 --> 00:24:31,716
Part b is type B. Pause the video and try it. Now we add a fourth covariate, x 4. What can you say about the VIFs for beta 1, beta 2 and beta 3?

116
00:24:32,166 --> 00:24:38,822
Step 1 of card B: take VIF 1. It is 1 over 1 minus R 1 squared.

117
00:24:39,272 --> 00:24:54,824
Step 2: what changes? Before, R 1 squared came from g 1 23, the regression of x 1 on x 2 and x 3. It was 0.2497. The blue point shows VIF 1.

118
00:24:55,274 --> 00:25:04,021
After, R 1 squared comes from the regression of x 1 on x 2, x 3 and x 4. It has one more covariate.

119
00:25:04,471 --> 00:25:13,623
Step 3: least squares can put 0 on x 4 and keep the old fit. So the new minimum of SSRes is at most the old one.

120
00:25:14,073 --> 00:25:27,257
SSTotal of x 1 does not change. So the new R 1 squared, 1 minus SSRes over SSTotal, is at least 0.2497.

121
00:25:27,707 --> 00:25:40,336
Step 4: 1 over 1 minus r increases with r. So the new VIF 1 is at least 1.3328. The arrow shows that the point can only move right.

122
00:25:40,786 --> 00:25:54,226
The same steps hold for beta 2 and beta 3. So VIF 2 is at least 3.3967, and VIF 3 is at least 3.4566.

123
00:25:54,676 --> 00:26:04,425
So the VIFs of beta 1, beta 2 and beta 3 stay the same or increase. This is the solution on slide 39.

124
00:26:04,875 --> 00:26:21,003
Slide 27 asks: is VIF j the ratio of the variance of beta j hat in the multiple regression to the variance of the slope in the simple regression of y on x j alone? Pause the video and think about it.

125
00:26:21,453 --> 00:26:31,928
Look at the real estate data. The simple regression of price on SqrFeet gives one standard error and one sigma hat. The full model gives others.

126
00:26:32,378 --> 00:26:48,165
The ratio of the squared standard errors is 33.43 over 23.27, which is 1.437. The VIF is 1.479. So the VIF is a different number.

127
00:26:48,615 --> 00:27:08,711
The reason: sigma squared means something different in each model. In the simple regression, the error includes the effects of Bedrooms and Lotsize. And the estimates of sigma squared are also different: 78,294 and 77,169.

128
00:27:09,161 --> 00:27:25,758
The slide gives the correct statement: the VIF is the variance of beta j hat, divided by the variance of beta j star hat. beta star hat is the least squares estimator with an idealized design matrix, X star.

129
00:27:26,208 --> 00:27:47,691
The idealized design matrix X star must satisfy three conditions. One: its column j is x j. Two: it has the same column space as X, so it gives the same fitted values and the same sigma hat squared. Three: its column j is uncorrelated with its other columns.

130
00:27:48,141 --> 00:28:05,421
Slide 28 builds such a matrix. Keep x j as column j. The blue arrow shows x j. For every other covariate x l, use the residuals of the regression of x l on x j. We call them e l given j.

131
00:28:05,871 --> 00:28:14,831
The black arrow is x l. Its fitted value from the regression on x j, x hat l, is the green part along x j.

132
00:28:15,281 --> 00:28:31,131
The residual e l given j is x l minus x hat l. It is the orange arrow, perpendicular to x j. Residuals are uncorrelated with the covariate of their regression. So condition 3 holds.

133
00:28:31,581 --> 00:28:44,701
x j and e l given j span the same plane as x j and x l. So the column space does not change, and condition 2 holds. Condition 1 holds by construction.

134
00:28:45,151 --> 00:28:58,869
Question type C uses the idealized design matrix. Practice question 1 a asks you to check the VIF with it, and part b asks you to compare fitted values and confidence intervals.

135
00:28:59,319 --> 00:29:04,417
Step 1 of method card C: keep x j as column j of X star.

136
00:29:04,867 --> 00:29:13,699
Step 2: for each other covariate x l, regress it on x j. Its residuals, e l given j, replace x l.

137
00:29:14,149 --> 00:29:25,541
Step 3: fit y on X star. The column space is the same, so the fitted values, sigma hat, and the confidence intervals for the mean response are the same.

138
00:29:25,991 --> 00:29:40,434
Step 4: VIF j is the variance of beta j hat over the variance of beta j star hat. Both models have the same sigma hat, so this is the ratio of the squared standard errors.

139
00:29:40,884 --> 00:29:54,260
Practice question 1 a is type C. Pause the video and try it. Fit a second model with the idealized design matrix, and check that VIF 1 is the ratio of the two variances.

140
00:29:54,710 --> 00:29:59,574
Step 1 of card C: keep the column of ones and x 1, SqrFeet.

141
00:30:00,024 --> 00:30:17,688
Step 2: replace Bedrooms by e 2 given 1, the residuals of Bedrooms on SqrFeet. Replace Lotsize by e 3 given 1, the residuals of Lotsize on SqrFeet. Both residual columns have correlation 0 with SqrFeet.

