00:30:31,431 --> 00:30:41,329
Sigma hat is the same in both models, 77,168.55, because the column space is the same.

144
00:30:41,779 --> 00:30:51,913
Step 4: with X, the standard error is 5.7821, so the variance is 33.43. The bracket shows it.

145
00:30:52,363 --> 00:31:02,283
With X star, the standard error is 4.7547, so the variance is 22.61. The blue bar shows it.

146
00:31:02,733 --> 00:31:13,847
Divide: 33.43 over 22.61 is 1.479. This is VIF 1 from type A. The check holds.

147
00:31:14,297 --> 00:31:22,489
Part b. How do the fitted values compare between the two models? And their confidence intervals? Pause the video and try it.

148
00:31:22,939 --> 00:31:39,153
Step 3 of card C. Plot the fitted values of the X star model against those of the X model. All 521 points lie on the line of equality. The largest difference is less than 10 to the minus 6 dollars.

149
00:31:39,603 --> 00:31:59,485
The table gives the 95 percent confidence intervals for the mean response at the first three houses. Both models give the same intervals. For house 1, both give 385,978 to 406,177 dollars.

150
00:31:59,935 --> 00:32:17,941
The reason: both design matrices have the same column space. So they have the same hat matrix H, the same fitted values, the same sigma hat squared, and the same confidence intervals for the mean response. Only the individual coefficients change.

151
00:32:18,391 --> 00:32:35,052
To recap. Card A computes a VIF: pick x j, regress it on the other covariates, read R j squared, and take 1 over 1 minus R j squared. The standard error grows by the square root of the VIF.

152
00:32:35,502 --> 00:32:48,601
Card B reasons about VIFs. R j squared is at least 0 and less than 1, and more covariates cannot make it smaller. 1 over 1 minus r increases with r.

153
00:32:49,051 --> 00:33:08,187
Card C uses the idealized design matrix: keep x j, and replace each other covariate by its residuals on x j. The fitted values, sigma hat and the confidence intervals stay the same, and the VIF is the ratio of the squared standard errors.

154
00:33:08,637 --> 00:33:31,506
And the facts behind the cards: X transpose e is 0. Perfect multicollinearity leaves X transpose X with no inverse. Correlated covariates inflate the standard errors of individual coefficients, but not of beta 1 plus beta 2, mean responses or predictions. This is the end of video 22.
